  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 AM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nForecast remains mostly consistent. We have southwest flow over\nour area with a longwave trough set up over the west coast. A \nstrong circulation over central California will break up today. \nA large chunk of that circulation will eject towards our region \ntoday. As it moves towards our area, it will interact with the \nmonsoon moisture and produce widespread showers with isolated to\nscattered thunderstorms. The track of the low has consistently \nbeen through the Magic Valley. As a result, the Magic Valley may\nsee more showers and less thunderstorm activity. Along the \nedges of that track, scattered storms look likely. Eastern \nIdaho, especially the eastern highlands has been consistently \nfavored for heavier rainfall amounts from storms with up to 0.50\ninches possible this afternoon. Convection and qpf amounts \nacross the central mountains is a bit of a question mark with \nhow much the mid and upper level clouds from the low suppress \ndevelopment. A flash flood watch remains in effect for the Big \nRock Fire east of Idaho Falls where combination of steep \nterrain, the effects of the recent burn, and the potential for \nheavy rain exist. Most of the activity will move into Montana \nthis evening, but a few isolated showers may persist through the\nnight.\n\nOn Friday, a second impulse arrives from the southwest. This second \nsystem isn't nearly as strong. As a result, coverage isn't as \nwidespread. Models are favoring the mountains and highlands for the \nbest chances for activity on Friday, especially the central \nmountains which are closer to the storm track. Locally heavy \nrainfall will remain a threat as the monsoonal moisture isn't going \nanywhere. \n\nOn Saturday, one final impulse works through the region. The best \nchances for storms is across the central mountains as the flow \nbegins to flatten from southwesterly to westerly. Isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms will remain possible areawide \nlargely due to the monsoon moisture. Potentially, could see a \nlimited flash flood threat once again Saturday. But the convection \nis beginning to shift from organized (good idea of where convection \nwill be) to disorganized (random). \n\nThe forecast for Sunday into early next week looks largely dry. But, \nthe moisture is still in place. Dew points will still be sitting in \nthe mid 40s to low 50s across the region, which is high for our \narea. Think at least isolated heat of the day pop up storms will be \naround. The cap associated with the ridge is rarely strong enough to \ncompletely suppress convection when the moisture is as high as it is.\n\nDaytime highs look well below normal for the remainder of the week \nand will not approach normal until early to mid next week.",
